Z||000000||mrfiero||03-08-2000||01:07 AM||||It seems as though GM...namely Pontiac... is getting a little particular these days about using their name and the Fiero name.  Tim, from Fiero Parts & Appraisals, got a phone call today from someone at GM stating that if he continues to use the Pontiac and Fiero names  in conjunction to sell anything that he would have to pay a special licensing fee.<P>I have no idea why GM would try this....afterall, it's been almost 12 years since they quit making the Fiero!<P>Has anyone else who sell parts on the internet been contacted by GM? <BR>  ||||reg||8
Z||000001||Gokart||03-08-2000||05:44 AM||||The way Pontiac has abandoned the Fiero I not very fond of them nor GM. I've had to hear it through two dealers that they just don't give a hoot about my car. I sometimes think we should all blacken out the Pontiac in out GT lights, remove their symbol from coupe sail windows and bondo their etch lettering in the drivers side headlight door. We should disguise out cars from ANY Pontiac/GM advertising. Why not!||||reg||8
Z||000002||fiero56||03-08-2000||08:25 AM||||Thats just what they want us to do. We can't give in, we need to make all the Pontiac names and symbols even larger, so there is no mistaking it for a Pontiac. Then, when at the dealership, park right in front of the showroom window.<P>I have never had problems with the Pontiac dealer I go to. The guys never say anything negative(or positive) but I an nerver hassled or given a hard time.  I just think they don't know what it is. The bay doors are never open for them to get a look. But I do park mine right in fron of the showroom, and it gets looks(when it's clean).||||reg||12
