Z||000010||Raydar||03-08-2000||10:57 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ChadMan:<BR><B>OK, I'm calm now.  I guess my logical position is this:  Either Pontiac is proud of the Fiero, and wants to protect it through licensing and trademarks, or Pontiac is ashamed of the Fiero, in which case the name belongs to anyone who loves the car enough to make parts and accessories.  They can't have it both ways!<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>&lt;snip&gt;<P>But that doesn't mean thay won't try. <BR>I'm reminded of the words of a former GM president...<P>"GM is NOT in the business of making cars. GM is in the business of making money."<P>(I remember seeing the "screwed GT" T-shirt in FOCOA's catalog. Might have to invest...) <P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||terryk||03-08-2000||12:15 PM||||Unfortunate as it may be, GM does own both names and if someone is making money off  them and GM isn't, they are well within their rights to get a piece of the action. I think that any of us would do the same thing if the situation was reversed. I've been pirated and while it's flattering, it also hurts my wallet. The dollar amount is irrelevant.<P>Has anyone actually asked what the licensing costs are? It's usually a percentage. <P>Another myth is that GM wants to hide the Fiero. While some dealers and non-GM affiliations like to say this, it is not true from GM's standpoint. Most people that I've talked with at GM still stand behind the car and are quite proud that so many of us continue to keep them going. They watch the replacement parts demand closely and either continue building the parts or release the tooling and specifications to the outside. <P>Terry<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 03-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Richard Parnell||03-08-2000||01:44 PM||||The rare times I do go to the dealership I also park my car rght in front of the show room.  It's a nice looking gold color coupe and the salesmen all come out to look at it while I'm picking up the parts.||||reg||6
Z||000013||Raydar||03-08-2000||05:27 PM||||TerryK. Of course, you are right about the licensing issue. It's just that what we, as Fiero owners, perceive is very frequently just the opposite of what the folks at GM say. Parts are either obscenely expensive, or no longer available, at all. Try to buy a steering rack or a coolant crossover pipe for an 88, lately? Thank God for the aftermarket, because GM sure isn't building this stuff any more. If GM stands behind the Fiero, they should offer resto parts on their websites. Not everybody drives a 69 Camaro. (If any of you do, more power to you, though.) <P>While I'm ranting... Why don't they offer the 3.4 crate motor with the starter holes drilled for the FWD configuration? This would greatly increase the sales of these engines. The do-it-yourselfers (and not just the Fiero folks) would be more likely to buy them, since they wouldn't have to have the holes drilled. <P>Whew! I need a Tylenol.<BR>  <p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 03-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||03-08-2000||07:10 PM||||Yes, This stinks. It could seriously complicate things for the entire community. I haven't seen all the details, so this post is in general discussion of the issue.<P>Actually I'm suprised it took this long for GM to start whining about this. In much as I Really don't like GM, they are perfectly within thier rights. Pontiac, Fiero, and The Pegasus logo, are all trademarked and/or copyrighted material.<P>While the U.S. hard document Copyright laws recognize something called "fair use" some countries don't. "Fair use" is what legally lets you copy a CD to cassette or MP3 and play it for your own use.<P>To make this even more fun there is now the "Millenium Digital Copyright Act.(I'm not sure I got name right.)" This is the law that is being used to make fair use more dificult by fighting both the MP3 format and software to play DVD's on Linux.<P>In this case however use of the GM materials is for a comercial use. That is entirely not legal under all existing copyright laws/treaties. There are a few ways around part of it, but the rules even for that are kind of tough.||||reg||1
Z||000015||terryk||03-08-2000||07:17 PM||||Hi Raydar,<P>I understand your frustration and I agree that the downfall of an 88 is the cost and availability of parts. I have an 86 and I find those parts to be reasonably priced. However, I also find that far more parts are available than many dealers tell you. Yes, they lie. Period. They don't want to look them up. "Can't get that anymore". <P>At the same time, I don't see GM's obligation to support such a low volume demand. There are quite a few more Camaro's than Fiero's. It's economy of scale and the Fiero just ain't got it.<P>As for the 3.4L not being drilled for the FWD appliations, it's my understanding that they are just dumping the excess block castings from the 94-96 Camaro. If they had all been consumed in production, they wouldn't have been offered for the "upgrade". They never planned on changing the FWD casting tools for that block. The 3.4L non-DOHC is the FWD casting and has what we want. Obviously the DOHC has no lifter gallery so fat lot of help that thing is. <P>Again, I understand your point. It *is* too bad that the Fiero didn't get produced long enough to find its way into the restoration market like the Camaro and Firebird's. But GM is the company that spent millions of dollars making the Pontiac and Fiero names mean something so they get the bucks when we use the names for profit.<P>I guess I'm not clear as to why GM should allow public domain use of their registered trademark because we can't get parts cheap.<P>I don't think GM is telling people they can't make parts for the Fiero. They are saying that the parts can't have registered tradmark names on them without GM getting a piece of the action. Anyone can make a pipe, rebuild a rack, cast a new head and not pay GM a dime. Now putting the FIERO name on it is different.<P>Terry<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 03-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||87GT||03-08-2000||09:25 PM||||I dont think we can blame Pontiac for the fiero cancelation.  I have read in many old articles that GM made the decision to kill fiero and that Pontiac Officials Vehemently protested that move.  I cant see why Pontiac would want to kill the fiero since they were the ones that pushed GM to fund the project.  I like GM sometimes but when I think of the fiero story, I really hate them.  I think its funny that they need some licence to use the Pontiac and Fiero name.  I say this becuase they try to ignore the thought that they had a fiero.  what a bunch a crapola||||reg||1
Z||000017||mrfiero||03-08-2000||09:52 PM||||Wow!  A lot of replies!  I fully understand it is well within GM's right to enforce the copyright/trademark that they own.  Unfortunately it could escalate into something much more.  Right now they are only going after businesses that use the Pontiac Fiero name to sell stuff, but will stop them from barring other uses of the name or logo?<P>Even this webpage uses the Fiero name and even has the Fiero crest at the top of every page....what if GM puts their foot down and says "no more until you pay us".     <P>That is the gist of my argument....I mean they effectively abandoned the Fiero at birth and now that they see there is still a market for the Fiero they want to grab more $$ out of our pockets.  That just plain stinks.<P>Hopefully it doesn't go any further, but there is a real possibility that it will.<P>Tim demanded that Pontiac send him the info on company letterhead (they wanted to fax it) so he can look it over and ensure it is actually from GM/Pontiac.  I'll let everyone know what they have to say when he gets the letter.   ||||reg||4
Z||000018||RossT||03-09-2000||12:36 AM||||About 8 years ago the MOPAR people went thru the same thing. You could not have the word<BR>"mopar" or use the "pentastar" symbol on any kind of magazine or advertisement.  The Mopar magazine people all pay a lisc. fee to<BR>mopar so they can use the name: Mopar Action, Mopar collector guide, H.P. Mopar etc... Many of the small aftermarket and reproduction people(equivalent to our Tom, Dave and SuperX) were harrased over and over again. It really pissed alot of people off. I don't follow Mopar as much any more, so I don't know how it all turned out.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Oreif||03-09-2000||02:24 AM||||Pontiac didn't kill the Fiero GM did. GM corporate office wanted to make the vans (transport, lumina, stilletto) and Chevy did complain about the dip in Corvette sales. I remember someone saying the 2-seater market was dropping off. I don't know if it was really GM or just a rumor. I do know GM cut the Advertising money in 1988. The deal was they had to sell 30,000 Fieros per year and in 1988 (with limited advertising) they still sold 26,000! If you ask me the dip in corvette sales was due to the lack of design changes and no Hi-Perf engines! The mid-80's Vette was one of the slowest ones made!||||reg||1
Z||000020||TooLowFiero||03-09-2000||03:04 AM||||well i just tried to search the pontiac website for Fiero  and guess what?  nothing<BR>i did find it in the history and i ALSO had to type in the 4 myself to find it i think that pontiac and GM for the most part need to get their heads outta their a$$'s and wake to see that the fiero is here and always will be us PROUD fiero owners love our car's and they just cant see it if we could get a petition or something for them to just start making fiero parts again i think that they will either blow us off or actually realize that we care about our cars and dont want to have to go to some junk yard for a part  well enough of my rambling<P>oh yeah BTW i own and 84 se and a 86 2m4<P>TooLow||||reg||1
Z||000021||Monkeyman||03-09-2000||04:33 AM||||terryk--You can't make an exact duplicate of something that has been patented with out infringing on the patent holders rights.  (Sorry to use you for an example, but your post was convenient! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)  It's perfectly acceptable to rebuild something, but GM/Pontiac holds the patent to the head (or whatever else you want to produce).  This is where allot of the kit car companies got into trouble when they tried to copy Ferraris.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Phil||03-09-2000||07:38 AM||||Actually kit car co.s only got into trouble when they used the name or the logo. Ferrari or the prancing horse on their cars. A copy of the Calfornia GT 350 is still being made just no F word or horse.||||reg||1
Z||000023||ChadMan||03-09-2000||09:33 AM||||Most of the folks who provide us Fieroholics with aftermarket parts and services are in it because it's their passion - not for the money (there are a lot easier ways to get rich than making Fiero parts!).<P>I'm no Communist, but it just strikes me as wrong when a juggernaut like GM's legal deparment targets some poor guy who's trying to fill a void that GM created itself by ignoring Fiero owners.<P>If GM is worried about maximizing its profits, it should concentrate on building better cars, not on squeezing nickels and dimes from people who serve the Fiero community.||||reg||8
